            "Article 226 gives High Courts a wider writ jurisdiction than the Supreme Court under Article 32, because High Courts can issue writs not only for Fundamental Rights but for 'any other purpose' (i.e., ordinary legal rights too). Unlike Article 32, the right to approach a High Court is not itself a Fundamental Right."),
        Article("Article 243", "Panchayati Raj", "Part IX", listOf("Local Government"),
            "Provides for a three-tier system of Panchayats at the village, intermediate and district levels, with regular elections, reservation of seats, and a State Finance Commission.",
            "Part IX, added by the 73rd Amendment (1992), gave constitutional status to rural local self-government. It mandates elections every five years, reservation for SCs, STs and women (at least one-third), and the establishment of State Election Commissions and State Finance Commissions."),
        Article("Article 280", "Finance Commission", "Part XII", listOf("Finance"),
            "The President shall constitute a Finance Commission every five years to recommend the distribution of tax revenues between the Union and the States.",
            "The Finance Commission is a quasi-judicial body that addresses vertical and horizontal fiscal imbalances by recommending how taxes should be shared between the Centre and the States and among the States themselves."),
        Article("Article 300A", "Right to Property", "Part XII", listOf("Property"),
            "No person shall be deprived of his property save by authority of law.",
            "Originally a Fundamental Right (Articles 19(1)(f) and 31), the right to property was removed from Part III by the 44th Amendment (1978) and made a constitutional/legal right under Article 300A. The State can now acquire property by law, but the action must still be lawful and non-arbitrary."),
        Article("Article 324", "Election Commission of India", "Part XV", listOf("Elections"),
            "The superintendence, direction and control of elections to Parliament, State Legislatures, and the offices of President and Vice-President shall vest in an Election Commission.",
            "Article 324 establishes the Election Commission of India (ECI) as an independent constitutional body responsible for conducting free and fair elections. It is currently a multi-member body (Chief Election Commissioner and Election Commissioners)."),
        Article("Article 352", "National Emergency", "Part XVIII", listOf("Emergency"),
            "If the President is satisfied that the security of India is threatened by war, external aggression or armed rebellion, a Proclamation of National Emergency may be made.",
            "A National Emergency under Article 352 can be declared on grounds of war, external aggression, or armed rebellion (the term 'internal disturbance' was replaced by 'armed rebellion' by the 44th Amendment, 1978). It must be approved by both Houses within one month. The most controversial use was the 1975-77 Emergency."),
        Article("Article 356", "President's Rule (State Emergency)", "Part XVIII", listOf("Emergency"),
